# Next steps for {{ workspace_name }}

This template separates **inputs** (what you load and keep loading) from
**model lifecycle** (per-model iterative work).

## Setup (now)

- [ ] `git init && git add -A && git commit -m "feat(stage-0): workspace bootstrap"`
- [ ] Create the venv: `uv venv .venv`
- [ ] **Activate it** in your shell: `source .venv/bin/activate`
      *(every new shell needs this; subsequent commands assume the venv is active)*
- [ ] Install workspace deps: `uv pip install -e ".[dev]"`
- [ ] Lint: `python scripts/lint-workspace.py` should print `workspace lint: OK`

> **Push to GitHub later.** The canonical convention is to push *after* you've
> registered at least one model — that way the first GitHub commit already shows
> real structure. See the **Publish** section below.

## Workspace inputs (load anytime)

A research project keeps loading new inputs throughout its life. None of these
are sequential stages — add and refresh whenever a new dataset, paper, or
expert document arrives.

- **Imports** — external models you reference, fork from, or operate against in-place
- **Models** — your own composite models (each is a git submodule; `gh repo create` + `git submodule add` from terminal)
- **Datasets** — experimental data each model compares against
- **References** — paper PDFs (drop a PDF; metadata auto-extracted via pypdf)
- **Expert knowledge** — curated PDFs, lab notes, expert-review docs

All five panels are in the dashboard's "Workspace inputs" section. Each + Add
button creates a new feature branch you review and PR.

### Imports (optional)

If your project builds on an existing model, register it in one of three modes:

- **reference** — read-only submodule under `external/<name>/`
- **fork-source** — catalog-only seed for a future `/pbg-add-model --from-import`
- **in-place** — submodule under `models/<name>/` with `external: true`

```
# plugin path
/pbg-import-models <name> --source <url> --ref <ref> --mode reference

# no-plugin path
# Edit workspace.yaml.imports directly, then:
git submodule add <source> external/<name>
```

### Models (registration)

Each model lives under `models/<name>/` as a git submodule (its own GitHub repo).

- [ ] Scaffold from the model template in `pbg-superpowers/templates/model/`
- [ ] In that dir: `git init && git add -A && git commit -m "feat: model scaffold"`, then `gh repo create <org>/<name> --public --source=. --remote=origin --push`
- [ ] Back in the workspace: `git submodule add https://github.com/<org>/<name>.git models/<name>`
- [ ] Add the entry under `workspace.yaml.models.<name>` (`submodule_path`, `remote`, `pbg_processes: []`, `stages.add_model.status: complete`)
- [ ] `python scripts/lint-workspace.py` to confirm

## Publish — push the workspace to GitHub

Now that the workspace has at least one model, push to GitHub:

```
gh repo create <org>/{{ workspace_name }} --public --source=. --remote=origin --push
```

Collaborators clone with:

```
git clone https://github.com/<org>/{{ workspace_name }}.git
cd {{ workspace_name }}
git submodule update --init --recursive
```

## Per-model lifecycle (the real multi-step workflow)

Each registered model goes through:

### 1. Setup — observables + visualizations

Pick the model's **observables** (which state variables you'll track —
e.g. `chromosome.DnaA_count`) and the **visualizations** that render them
(time-series, phase-space, heatmap, histogram). One per addition; all live.

```
# plugin path
/pbg-add-observable <model>
/pbg-add-visualization <model>

# no-plugin path (dashboard)
bash scripts/serve.sh
# open the model's deep-dive page and use the + Add observable / + Add visualization buttons
```

### 2. Initial run (v0.2.1)

Run the composite with the configured observables; capture trajectories;
render the visualizations. Coming in v0.2.1 — for now, this section is a
placeholder in the per-model dashboard.

### 3. Mechanism integration (the guided iterative part)

Add mechanisms one at a time, each backed by an expert doc / dataset /
reference from your input bag. Each mechanism is a separate PR with:

- The mechanism's biological motivation (linked to its source artifact)
- Implementation tasks
- Acceptance tests
- Phase Gate evaluation

```
# Generate mechanism plan (dashboard or terminal)
bash scripts/add-phase-plan.sh

# Start a mechanism
bash scripts/start-phase.sh <model> <n>

# Evaluate the gate when done
bash scripts/evaluate-phase-gate.sh <model> <n>
```

This is where the dashboard's step-by-step guidance pays off — the
"carefully add mechanisms using curated knowledge" loop.

## Two paths

- **With Claude Code + the [pbg-superpowers plugin](https://github.com/vivarium-collective/pbg-superpowers):**
  every action is a `/pbg-*` skill.
- **Without the plugin:** the dashboard's `bash scripts/serve.sh` is the primary UI;
  or use the shell scripts in `scripts/` directly.

## Browser dashboard

Run:

    bash scripts/serve.sh

This renders the workspace dashboard at `reports/index.html` and serves it locally.
The top panel ("Next step") detects your current state from `workspace.yaml` and
shows the next action.

Per-model deep dives render at `models/<name>/reports/index.html`. The deep-dive
page has:

1. **Setup** — observables + visualizations panels (add + view)
2. **Initial run** — placeholder (v0.2.1)
3. **Mechanism integration** — the iterative phase tracker
4. **Reference** — process registry, type registry, composite document, run tests

Actions available in the browser:

- Register imports, datasets, references (PDF drop), expert documents
- Add observables and visualizations per model
- Generate mechanism plans, start mechanisms, evaluate gates
- Run pytest for any model and see output inline

Every action creates its own feature branch (e.g.
`stage/setup-observable-chromosome-rep1-DnaA-1715284800`) so you can review
the diff and open a PR from your terminal:

    gh pr create --base main --head <branch>

## Config files to know

- `workspace.yaml` — canonical state (models, imports, observables, visualizations, mechanisms)
- `docs/decisions.yaml` — append-only design log
- `references/{papers.bib, claims.yaml}` — bibliography + claim → paper mapping
- `.pbg/schemas/` — workspace + phase JSON schemas (used by `lint-workspace.py`)

## When stuck

1. Run `python scripts/lint-workspace.py` — most workspace-shape issues surface here.
2. Open the relevant `SKILL.md` in `pbg-superpowers/skills/`.
3. File an issue at <https://github.com/vivarium-collective/pbg-superpowers/issues>.
